## (7) the sampling diagnosis, in the order it was worked through

`RECORDER ok` proves the export exists in these bytes; the renderer emitted eight well-formed rows; the
remote digest matched; the buffer is the contract's 4096; both reader threads start before the gate
order. So the first read-back was NOT a broken channel — it was a poll that landed before the first
allocator event, and a next poll (2 ms later) that never happened because the child was already dead:

  - `MFREC 0 watch` showed 5 pristine slots + 3 `Mx` rows in the later runs, so the ring IS written on
    Windows; the empty-ring renders were simply taken earlier than the events.
  - The two readers' cadences bracket the window: the 0.2 ms `WITNESS` thread landed a sample after
    `new_rt MmMmMm` was written in every run, the 2 ms `MFREC` thread landed one in 5 of 84 runs. The
    death therefore follows the last allocator event by roughly a fraction of a millisecond to ~2 ms.
  - Remedy that added no code and no capability: oversubscribe the CPU so the scheduler preempts the
    sequence thread inside that window. The 14 runs taken sequentially or at light concurrency produced
    0 data-bearing renders; the oversubscribed batches (71 real child runs at 16–24-way concurrency)
    produced 5. The instrument was never changed — only when the existing reader got a timeslice.
  - Every data-bearing run died with the identical 8-RVA frame set, the identical witness text, and
    returncode 3, so the concurrency that made the read possible did not change the behaviour being
    read (see also (10): the trap site re-pins to the same source line on these bytes).

