Same function range (`0xbd70..0xbdf3`, 131 bytes), same source site (quickjs.c:1494:12), same 141 sites;
only the record's `.rdata` address moved (`0x5aa430` → `0x5ab430`). So Task 1 added a read point and moved
neither the trap nor its address.

What `MFREC` adds that `WITNESS` could not: the witness can only say *how many* allocation lifecycles
finished (`MmMmMm` = three `heap_malloc` calls, all returned, no fourth entered) and it caps at 8 tags;
§45.5's three candidates are questions about VALUES — a size, a pointer's low bits, which
`JSMallocState` drove the call. Those three numbers exist only in the ring, and (6) is the first time
this project has printed them.

Native positive control for the same recorder (Linux, `c2_diag_tests`, sequence completes): Task 1's
probe rendered `Mx size=fe8 ptr=2dc2e18 st=000002d9f7c0`, `Rx size=378 ptr=2dc50a8`,
`Fx size=268 ptr=2dc4e28`. Note the native `ptr` values are `≡ 8 (mod 16)` too — the Windows rows in (6)
have the same shape, which is the point of (10) below.

## (9) reading, judgment table applied per render

Parsed from the text with the literal tokens, 13 event rows across 5 renders; all 13 are `Mx`
(completed `heap_malloc`), none is `Fx`/`Rx`, none is an entered-but-not-exited `<kind>_`.

**Row 1 of the table (`size == 0` on an `M`/`R` row → candidate (b)).** Sizes observed, in order:
`0x5a0` (1440), `0x808` (2056), `0xff0` (4080) — four renders agree on exactly that triple, and the
fifth (stale, below) shows `0xff0`. No zero-size `M`/`R` row. **This is agreement with a fact already
established offline, not new evidence**: `__js_malloc(s, 0)` is served from the embedded
`zero_size_block` (`quickjs.c:1549-1556`) and `js_new_string8_len` short-circuits `len <= 0` (`:4018`),
so a zero-size request never reaches `mf.js_malloc` on this vendored revision. Candidate (b) is
therefore NOT "excluded by this run"; it was excluded before it, and this run is consistent with that.
The 5 pristine `__ size=0` rows in every render are never-written slots, not zero-size requests, and the
table's row 1 reads only `M`/`R` rows — they cannot enter the test.

**Row 2 (`ptr` alignment → candidate (a)).** Evaluated as **`ptr % 8 != 0`**, the reading mandated by
`JS_MALLOC_ALIGN = 8` (`quickjs.c:243`) and `offsetof(JSMallocBlockHeader, user_data) == 8` (`:270-280`),
because UBSan's misalignment check on `JSMallocBlockHeader` fires iff a pointer is not 8-aligned. All 13
pointers have `ptr % 8 == 0`, so candidate (a) is not selected. The observed set of `ptr % 16` is
exactly `{0x8}` — every pointer we hand back is `≡ 8 (mod 16)`, which is the structural consequence of
`AllocHeader` being one `size_t` and `heap_malloc` returning `h + 1` on a 16-aligned `HeapAlloc` block
(`quickjs_wrapper.c:161-163, :312`). Per that same ruling, `ptr & 0xf != 0` on its own is NOT evidence
for (a): the native (unbroken) sequence renders the same `≡ 8 (mod 16)` shape, so the plan's second
clause — "原生同一序列全是 16 对齐" — is measured false by this read-back. What the data does support is
narrower and worth keeping straight: **every pointer our callbacks returned is 8-aligned, i.e. satisfies
what QuickJS's own header arithmetic requires.** The panic we are chasing is
`member access within NULL POINTER`, a nullness trip, not a misalignment trip; this run says our returned
pointers are not the null source.

**Row 3 (`st` → candidate (c), split `JSMallocState`).** Distinct `st` **per complete render = 2**
(count printed, as required, because the plan's wording "某条记录的 `st` 与 `JS_NewRuntime2` 那次 `st` 不同"
would fire on a healthy runtime too): `quickjs.c:2070` declares `JSMallocState ms` as a STACK local,
`:2076 rt = mf->js_malloc(&ms, sizeof(JSRuntime))` carries that stack address, and only `:2082
rt->malloc_ctx.malloc_state = ms` makes the heap copy every later call carries. Two values in one
`JS_NewRuntime2` are structurally required. The observed pattern is exactly the expected-healthy one:

    render  row1 st            row2/row3 st      row1 ptr        (st − row1 ptr)
    ov3     0x6dec18           0x154be70         0x154ba68       +0x408
    C3      0x6dec18           0x181f790         0x181f388       +0x408
    C15     0x6dec18           0x17e7530         0x17e7128       +0x408
    C22     0x6dec18           0x17c6bc0         0x17c67b8       +0x408

  - the first value is **identical across four different child processes** (`0x6dec18`) — a stack
    address, which is where a per-process-independent value belongs;
  - the second value moves with the heap and equals the runtime block pointer **+ 0x408 = 1032**, and
    1032 is the computed `offsetof(JSMallocContext, malloc_state)` with `offsetof(JSRuntime, malloc_ctx)`
    = 0 (`arena_list[31] + free_arena_list[31]` = 992, `zero_size_block[8]` = 1000, `mf` (4 pointers) =
    1032) — measured from this TU with `zig cc`: `sizeof(JSRuntime)=1440`, and `JSRuntime` contains no
    `long`/`time_t`/`wchar_t`, so the number carries to the LLP64 target;
  - the stack value never reappears after the runtime row, and the runtime-sized row (`0x5a0` =
    `sizeof(JSRuntime)`) is present in every complete render.

So candidate (c) is not selected — and not by absence: the healthy 2-value pattern with the +0x408
identity was positively observed, four times, in four different address spaces.

**The fifth render is a stale one, and pooling renders would have fabricated candidate (c).**
`MFREC 0 watch` in `C5` prints a single `Mx size=ff0` row in the third slot with slots 0 and 1 rendered
pristine. No sequential ring history can produce that: with three events the cursor is 3 and the walk is
5 pristine + 3 events (the other four renders). It is the documented limit of reading the cursor ONCE
before the walk (`quickjs_wrapper.c:666-671`) while the writer advances: the cursor was read at 0, and by
the time the walk reached slot 2 the third event had landed, while slots 0 and 1 had already been read as
pristine. The render therefore shows an ordering that was never a ring state — and if its `st` were
pooled with the complete renders, the distinct-`st` count would rise from 2 to 6 and the table's row 3
would "select" candidate (c) purely from a reader artifact. Classification is per render; the stale one
contributes nothing except corroboration that its one row is `≡ 8 (mod 16)` and `size > 0`.

**Which row of the table the data selects: the fourth.** No `M`/`R` row has `size == 0` (row 1 — and per
the ruling above that fact was already established offline, so it is not booked as this run's exclusion);
every returned pointer is 8-aligned (row 2 fails); the `st` pattern is the healthy two-value one with
the runtime copy at +0x408 (row 3 fails). The three §45.5 candidates are therefore not the explanation,
and by the plan's own instruction the candidate table is declared incomplete: the raw `MFREC` text and
the last record before the death go back to the human and **this plan stops — no fourth candidate is
invented here.**

The last record before the death, in all four complete renders, is the same event:

    Mx size=ff0 ptr=1884f28 st=00000154be70        (ov3; +0x408 identity holds in each render)

**What those three rows are**, read off the vendored source after the fact (arithmetic, not observation):
`0x5a0` = `sizeof(JSRuntime)` = the `mf->js_malloc(&ms, sizeof(JSRuntime))` at `:2076`; `0x808` =
`js_malloc_large`'s `sizeof(JSMallocLargeBlockHeader) (8) + 2048` = the `js_mallocz_rt(sizeof(uint32_t) *
512)` inside `JS_ResizeAtomHash(rt, 512)`, the first thing `JS_InitAtoms` does (`:3180`); `0xff0` =
4080 = `sizeof(JSMallocArena) (40) + 101 * 40` — the new-arena request of the **40-byte block class**,
which is unique to that class among the 31 (`js_malloc_block_sizes`). In `JS_InitAtoms` order that is:
runtime, atom hash, then the arena created by the FIRST `__JS_NewAtomInit`'s name-string request
(`js_alloc_string_rt(rt, len, 0)` at `:3430`). §43 parked the death exactly between ":3430 名字串分配" and
":3330 atom_array 增长", and §45 pinned the trap inside `__JS_NewAtomInit`; the ring now shows the death
sitting **after the 40-byte arena was handed over successfully** and before any further `mf` call — the
`atom_array` growth (which would have been a `0x1640` large-block row) is absent from every render.
The identification of rows 2 and 3 by name is arithmetic plus call order, not an observed label; it is
stated as such and belongs in the boundaries below.

## (10) read-only self-attestation

    - Nothing was injected. No CreateRemoteThread, no hook/Interceptor/patchCode/writeByteArray/
      Memory.protect, no Frida session, no read or write of any game process's memory.
    - stellaris.exe was not started, not contacted, not even opened from disk by these commands;
      §44's single-shot dispatch authorization stays spent and was not used or rehearsed.
    - The only processes that loaded the DLL were child pythons that scripts/c2_selfabort_repro.py
      spawned and owns; 84 of them died with status 3 and none printed SURVIVED, so no behaviour change
      was introduced by the recorder's presence (the plan's Step 3 stop-condition was never reached).
    - The DLL bytes were not modified by this task: no src/ edit, no new export, no new probe; the
      measurement came from re-running an existing tool, and the only thing varied was host load.
    - scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py read a file copy of the DLL. The struct sizes/offsets were computed by
      compiling the vendored quickjs.c into a throwaway /tmp probe — no repository file, no shipped
      artifact involved.
    - C:\temp\sqjs_s43.dll (§45's named artifact) was neither overwritten nor reused; the new bytes went
      to C:\temp\sqjs_s46.dll.
